<script lang="ts">
	import { Scrolly, Step } from '$lib/scrolly';
	import { MapStage } from '$lib/stages';
	import { streetsStyle } from '$lib/styles';
	import Em from '$lib/ui/Em.svelte';
	import Counter from './Counter.svelte';
	import Explorer from './Explorer.svelte';
	import PostalLayers from './PostalLayers.svelte';
	import { postal } from './postal-data.svelte';
	import {
		ADDRESSES,
		BLOCK_542264,
		BLOCKS_264,
		BUILDINGS,
		DISTRICTS,
		EXPLORER_START,
		HEATMAP,
		HOME,
		SECTOR_54,
		SECTOR_54_264,
		type PostalView
	} from './views';

	// The Explorer moves the map as the reader types, through its Step's View.
	let explorer: PostalView = $state(EXPLORER_START);
</script>

Singapore's postal code system not only aids mail delivery but also offers a unique view of the city's layout and organization. It's like a hidden map showing how the island is structured and divided.

This small visualization illustrates Singapore's postal code system and is inspired by Ben Fry's [zipdecode](https://benfry.com/zipdecode/).

Scroll down to learn more!

<Scrolly layout="overlay" label="Map of Singapore's postal codes">
{#snippet stage(view, progress)}
<MapStage {view} {progress} style={streetsStyle} duration={3000}>
<PostalLayers {view} />
</MapStage>
{/snippet}

<Step view={{ camera: HOME, layers: DISTRICTS }} />

<Step view={{ camera: HOME, layers: DISTRICTS }}>

Beginning in the 1950s, Singapore was originally split into <Em color="#206095">28</Em> Postal Districts.

</Step>

<Step view={{ camera: HOME, layers: HEATMAP, pattern: '*' }}>

Fast forward to September 1995, the postal code uses a <Em color="#206095">6</Em> digit system which divides Singapore into 80 postal sectors.

<Em color="#206095">{postal.total ?? '…'}</Em> postal codes are displayed on this map to illustrate its distribution.

</Step>

<Step view={{ camera: SECTOR_54, layers: HEATMAP, pattern: '54*' }}>

Singapore's 6 digit postal code system works as follows:

<h2><Em color="#003C57">54</Em><span>2264</span></h2>

The first two digits of each postal code denote a postal sector.

</Step>

<Step view={{ camera: BLOCKS_264, layers: BUILDINGS, pattern: '54[2-4]264' }}>

<h2>542<Em color="#003C57">264</Em></h2>

The last 3 digits are used to indicate residential properties in an apartment block.

</Step>

<Step view={{ camera: BLOCK_542264, layers: BUILDINGS, pattern: '542264' }}>

<h2>54<Em color="#003C57">2</Em>264</h2>

For residential blocks that share the same number, the 3rd digit is used to differentiate between blocks.

</Step>

<Step view={{ camera: SECTOR_54_264, layers: [...HEATMAP, ...ADDRESSES], pattern: '54*264' }}>

<h2>54<Em color="#003C57"><Counter /></Em>264</h2>

Within the same postal sector, postal codes are given out based on street names, from A to Z. In each area:

1. Streets starting with 'A' get codes first
2. Then streets starting with 'B', and so on
3. The third number in the code often matches something about the street name

This system is used for homes, shops, and factories.

</Step>

<Step view={EXPLORER_START}>

<h2>Try it yourself!</h2>

Scroll below to try some postal codes!

</Step>

<Step view={explorer}>
<Explorer bind:view={explorer} />
</Step>
</Scrolly>
